 "standard_text": "Understand and use composite functions and inverse functions and their graphs; the modulus of a linear function; solve equations involving the modulus.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Year 13 A level maths worksheets on functions: composite functions fg(x), inverse functions f⁻¹(x) and modulus equations. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "fg(x) means apply g first, then f.",
 "key_sub": "|x| = a means x = a or x = −a.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>fg(<i>x</i>) = f(g(<i>x</i>)): do g first. The inverse f<sup>−1</sup> undoes f: swap <i>x</i> and <i>y</i> and rearrange. Only one-to-one functions have inverses, and the graph of f<sup>−1</sup> is the reflection of f in <i>y</i> = <i>x</i>.</p><p>|<i>ax</i> + <i>b</i>| = <i>c</i> gives <i>ax</i> + <i>b</i> = <i>c</i> or <i>ax</i> + <i>b</i> = −<i>c</i>.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>f(<i>x</i>) = 2<i>x</i> + 1, g(<i>x</i>) = <i>x</i>². Find fg(3).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>g(3) = 9</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>f(9) = 19</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 19</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Solve |2<i>x</i> − 1| = 5.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<i>x</i> − 1 = 5 or −5</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: <i>x</i> = 3 or <i>x</i> = −2</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Doing f first in fg(x).</li><li>Writing f⁻¹(x) = 1/f(x).</li><li>Finding only one solution of a modulus equation.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is a composite function?",
   "a": "fg(x) means put x into g, then put the result into f."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does the modulus do?",
   "a": "|x| is the size of x without its sign, so |−3| = 3. Its graph is a V shape."
  }
 ],
